Church in one of Southern California's  wealthiest communities says it has  stopped serving dinners to the homeless  under pressure from the city the United  Methodist Church in Malibu recently  canceled its weekly meal program  organizers say officials in the seaside  community voiced concerns after  receiving public safety complaints Jamie  yuccas is at the church in Malibu Jamie  good morning good morning the homeless  meals are served at this church campus  but you can feed on the sign it's also  home to a nursery school directly behind  the church is a middle school and high  school city officials are concerned that  dozens of homeless people come here many  from outside communities Malibu  California is known worldwide for its  secluded beaches sun-drenched streets  and multi-million dollar homes but  living among Malibu's well-to-do are the  down-and-out for years volunteers at the  city's United Methodist Church have  pulled out food carts and set tables  serving as many as a hundred meals each  week to the homeless like Micah Johnson  they keep me accountable and the church  is really helpful keeping my spirits up  Reggie Coco's organization ran the  program until earlier this month he says  the city asked him to stop they were  trying to highly encourage us to use the  resources that they already have  Malibu's mayor says the city never  forced the program to end but he does  have serious concerns that homeless from  around LA County are taking the bus to  Malibu for the free meals  mayor skyler pete and what comes with  that would some of those people is drug  use is assault the Los Angeles Sheriff's  Department says homeless related service  calls including those reporting crimes  have more than tripled in the Malibu  area from 2011 to 2016 truth be told  I've never met any of these people that  are causing these problems it sounds  like you just don't want these people  here let's just move to the next town no  no we're doing a better job of getting  them resources and we're going to  continue to do that we're going to  continue to get better at it  city officials say they already work  with two full-time outreach workers that  connect the homeless with programs  including health services shelter and  substance abuse counseling but they  aren't currently handing out meals and  goko says his dinners provide a vital  connection that the city can't duplicate  to us it's more than just the dinners  it's not food it's not really the  relation it's the relationship it's the  mentorship that's that's where you get  people lit up from within and they  actually go out on their own  the city says one possible solution is  handing out box lunches around town in  other locations city leaders and  community leaders will meet once again  on Monday to discuss this meal program  flat Jamie yuccas thank you very much
 

